#ifndef __AXIS_HGLSL__
#define __AXIS_HGLSL__

// Z-up MC2 world frame <-> Y-up frame: (x, y, z) -> (-x, z, y).
//
// NAMING TRAP (it produced 8c498015c, which read the mech normal's .y as up): the shader sources call the Y-up
// frame (x = -east, y = up, z = north) "Stuff" (bone matrices, prop/mech shapeToWorld, IblShCoeffs) and the Z-up
// frame (x = east, y = north, z = elevation) "MC2 world" (what worldToClipGL consumes). The position and the
// normal of mc2ToRender(stuffVec) are therefore in the Z-up MC2 world frame: UP IS .z THERE.
// Pinned by tests/unit/test_mech_up_axis.cpp.
//
// WHY one conversion for both directions: the permutation is an involution. Applying it
// twice gives (-(-x), y, z) = (x, y, z), so the spelling that takes a vector of one frame
// to the other is also the one that brings it back. mc2ToRender / renderToMc2 carry the same body and exist
// so that a call site says which way it is going.
//
// The C++ twin is RenderCore/axis_convert.h and the matrix form is
// mc2xform::makeAxisSwapMC2toGL() (mclib/xform_conventions.cpp); a doctest holds all three
// to the same answer on the basis vectors. Do not re-type the triple at a call site:
// tools/axis_swizzle_ratchet.py counts hand-written copies and lets the count only fall.
vec3 mc2ToRender(vec3 v) { return vec3(-v.x, v.z, v.y); }
vec3 renderToMc2(vec3 v) { return mc2ToRender(v); }

#endif // __AXIS_HGLSL__
